WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.639
We begin with the brazen cyberattack that
has shut down the biggest pipeline in the

00:00:03.639 --> 00:00:04.639
United States.

00:00:04.639 --> 00:00:11.410
This is the biggest attack that involves vital
infrastructure.

00:00:11.410 --> 00:00:15.780
There are increasing concerns over the possibility
of shortages of gasoline and diesel.

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:21.449
It’s a pipeline that moves 45% of the east
coast diesel, gas, and jet fuel from Texas,

00:00:21.449 --> 00:00:22.920
all the way up to New Jersey.

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:28.070
On the night of May 6th, 2021, a critical
piece of American energy infrastructure was

00:00:28.070 --> 00:00:29.529
crippled by Russian hackers.

00:00:29.529 --> 00:00:34.190
The victim of the attack was the Colonial
Pipeline, which helps carry 3 million barrels

00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:37.420
of fuel per day between Texas and New York.

00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:42.820
One day later, executives at Colonial Pipeline
sent the hackers $5 million dollars in Bitcoin.

00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:47.559
The story of this particular hack is incredibly
interesting, but not for the reason you might

00:00:47.559 --> 00:00:48.559
think.

00:00:48.559 --> 00:00:50.600
There’s actually a lot more going on here.

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:54.540
The Colonial Pipeline cyberattack is a harbinger
of things to come, and the United States is

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:56.570
woefully underprepared.

00:00:56.570 --> 00:01:01.680
Major technological and economic forces have
shifted to make these attacks easier to execute

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:05.420
and much more profitable, and this is really
only the beginning.

00:01:05.420 --> 00:01:08.286
So let’s dig in and figure out what’s
going on.

00:01:08.286 --> 00:01:12.459
In order to understand what makes this attack
so unique, it’s important to examine the

00:01:12.459 --> 00:01:15.170
role that oil pipelines play in the American
economy.

00:01:15.170 --> 00:01:20.619
The U.S. is the third largest country in the
world, and the number one consumer of oil.

00:01:20.619 --> 00:01:26.719
Despite electric vehicles becoming increasingly
popular, 97% of cars still run on gasoline,

00:01:26.719 --> 00:01:31.729
and the process of transporting that fuel
all across the country relies on a massive

00:01:31.729 --> 00:01:33.140
network of pipelines.

00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:37.780
The Colonial Pipeline is the biggest of them
all and millions of Americans rely on it to

00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:43.909
get gasoline from refineries in Texas up the
east coast to cities like New York.

00:01:43.909 --> 00:01:48.170
The pipeline has been active since the 1960s
and, as you can guess, they don’t exactly

00:01:48.170 --> 00:01:50.560
operate like a modern technology company.

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:55.270
Computer systems are outdated and a report
from 2018 found glaring deficiencies in their

00:01:55.270 --> 00:01:56.530
security systems.

00:01:56.530 --> 00:02:01.349
In fact, the consultant who led the investigation
said that “an eighth grader could have hacked

00:02:01.349 --> 00:02:03.340
into the Colonial Pipeline system.”

00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:07.119
So when a group of Russian hackers set their
sights on the company, it looked like a gold

00:02:07.119 --> 00:02:08.119
mine.

00:02:08.119 --> 00:02:12.439
Colonial had plenty of resources to pay a
bounty and little security to fight back against

00:02:12.439 --> 00:02:13.810
an attack, so they went to work.

00:02:13.810 --> 00:02:18.129
We still don’t know exactly how the hackers
got access to the Colonial network, but once

00:02:18.129 --> 00:02:23.790
they did, the hackers quickly exported nearly
100Gb of data before encrypting everything

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:24.790
on the network.

00:02:24.790 --> 00:02:30.170
This strategy is at the core of every modern
ransomware attack: steal the data and then

00:02:30.170 --> 00:02:34.040
encrypt everything on the network to lock
your victim out of their own systems.

00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:38.040
This creates an impossible choice for victim
companies, and this is the first reason why

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:45.790
these ransomware attacks are becoming so prevalent:
attackers give victims no good options.

00:02:45.790 --> 00:02:49.900
The hackers weren’t actually able to exfiltrate
all 100 gbs of data.

00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:53.959
They couldn’t just send the data to Russia
directly, so they had to route through a string

00:02:53.959 --> 00:02:55.230
of proxy servers.

00:02:55.230 --> 00:02:59.930
US Officials from the National Security Agency
noticed the unusual activity and were able

00:02:59.930 --> 00:03:04.349
to intervene and shut down key servers while
the data was in still transit, but enough

00:03:04.349 --> 00:03:09.060
of the data made it to Russia that the Colonial
Pipeline team was still stuck between a rock

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:10.060
and a hard place.

00:03:10.060 --> 00:03:14.209
By stealing at least some of the data and
then encrypting the network, hackers trap

00:03:14.209 --> 00:03:16.459
companies in a lose-lose situation.

00:03:16.459 --> 00:03:21.480
If they try and restore their data or buy
new computers, the hackers can leak the stolen

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:27.040
data onto the darkweb, which will allow other
hackers to take advantage of customer information

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:29.489
or competitors to steal intellectual property.

00:03:29.489 --> 00:03:33.000
This is a terrible scenario, but the alternative
is sometimes worse.

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:37.540
If your company refuses to pay to decrypt
the data, valuable information could be lost

00:03:37.540 --> 00:03:42.150
forever and that can cause huge financial
losses.

00:03:42.150 --> 00:03:47.189
Rudimentary ransomware attacks do things as
simplistic as just renaming files, or altering

00:03:47.189 --> 00:03:51.510
them slightly to try and corrupt them, but
modern attacks like the one perpetrated against

00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:55.400
the Colonial Pipeline take a much more technologically
advanced approach.

00:03:55.400 --> 00:03:59.849
They employ state-of-the-art encryption methods
to ensure that it’s impossible for data

00:03:59.849 --> 00:04:00.849
to be recovered.

00:04:00.849 --> 00:04:04.290
The only option is often to simply pay the
ransom.

00:04:04.290 --> 00:04:08.930
And that leads to the second reason why these
attacks are becoming more common: cryptocurrency

00:04:08.930 --> 00:04:12.379
has enabled semi-anonymous payment across
borders.

00:04:12.379 --> 00:04:17.470
Viruses and malware have been around for decades,
but only in the past few years has it become

00:04:17.470 --> 00:04:22.110
possible to get a victim to pay you during
a ransomware attack and actually walk away

00:04:22.110 --> 00:04:23.710
with anything of value.

00:04:23.710 --> 00:04:29.310
Before bitcoin became popular, sending a wire
transfer across borders would require coordination

00:04:29.310 --> 00:04:34.280
with a foreign bank, and US government agents
could easily arrange to catch the perpetrator

00:04:34.280 --> 00:04:35.940
when they went to receive the payment.

00:04:35.940 --> 00:04:40.160
Banks that consistently aided criminals in
international schemes would be sanctioned

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:43.120
and eventually removed from the global payment
network.

00:04:43.120 --> 00:04:46.130
But crypto changes all of this.

00:04:46.130 --> 00:04:50.289
Victims can now easily buy bitcoin through
exchanges and transfer to the hacker’s wallet

00:04:50.289 --> 00:04:52.889
without leaving a significant paper trail.

00:04:52.889 --> 00:04:55.730
Sometimes all a victim needs is a credit card
and a web browser.

00:04:55.730 --> 00:04:59.789
In a funny twist, sometimes this winds up
benefiting the victim.

00:04:59.789 --> 00:05:04.539
A few years ago, when a major law firm was
the victim of a ransomware attack, they assumed

00:05:04.539 --> 00:05:08.760
they would have no option but to pay up, so
they bought several hundred thousand dollars

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:09.760
in bitcoin.

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:13.330
But they also retained a cyber security team
to investigate the incident.

00:05:13.330 --> 00:05:17.400
This team figured out that the attacker was
based in the United States and worked with

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:18.930
the FBI to apprehend him.

00:05:18.930 --> 00:05:23.120
They didn’t need to pay the ransom but they
still had the bitcoin, and in the few weeks

00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:27.330
that they were working to fight the hack,
they had made over a million dollars in profit.

00:05:27.330 --> 00:05:30.430
The Colonial Pipeline folks weren’t quite
so lucky.

00:05:30.430 --> 00:05:34.919
Within hours of the attack, they instantly
understood how serious the situation was and

00:05:34.919 --> 00:05:39.110
knew that they had to pay, so they bought
$5 million dollars in bitcoin and prepared

00:05:39.110 --> 00:05:40.430
to send it to the hackers.

00:05:40.430 --> 00:05:44.819
Now, big companies like Colonial have cyber
insurance policies, which will often reimburse

00:05:44.819 --> 00:05:49.380
ransoms like this one, but there is an interesting
cat and mouse game going on that is making

00:05:49.380 --> 00:05:51.780
these ransoms skyrocket in value recently.

00:05:51.780 --> 00:05:53.280
Here’s how it works:

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:57.370
Companies are worried about ransomware attacks,
so they go out and buy cybersecurity insurance.

00:05:57.370 --> 00:06:02.350
But hackers know that companies have insurance,
so they get more aggressive about targeting

00:06:02.350 --> 00:06:03.350
companies.

00:06:03.350 --> 00:06:07.240
Additionally, when a hacker breaches a corporate
network, they have access to all of the company's

00:06:07.240 --> 00:06:10.120
files, including their cyber insurance policy.

00:06:10.120 --> 00:06:14.600
The first thing some hackers do when they
infiltrate a company is search for documents

00:06:14.600 --> 00:06:18.880
relating to cyber insurance to find out exactly
what the company can afford to pay.

00:06:18.880 --> 00:06:23.580
If the hacker sees that the insurance company
will pay up to $5 million in ransomware fees,

00:06:23.580 --> 00:06:26.460
then they will demand exactly $5 million.

00:06:26.460 --> 00:06:30.419
I’m generally a big fan of cryptocurrencies,
but it’s clear that they are a big reason

00:06:30.419 --> 00:06:33.210
why ransomware attacks are growing in popularity.

00:06:33.210 --> 00:06:37.139
Hackers love that they can deploy these hacks
from the safety of a country like Russia and

00:06:37.139 --> 00:06:41.400
still get paid quickly and discreetly without
drawing the attention of authorities.

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:46.409
But there’s still one more big reason why
these attacks are spreading like wildfire.

00:06:46.409 --> 00:06:50.380
And this is reason number there: ransomware
has become a product.

00:06:50.380 --> 00:06:55.130
When you think of a typical hacker, you probably
think of a lone wolf coding long into the

00:06:55.130 --> 00:06:58.680
night in some basement, but that’s all changing.

00:06:58.680 --> 00:07:03.160
American authorities have identified a hacker
group by the name of DarkSide as the party

00:07:03.160 --> 00:07:07.639
responsible for the Colonial Pipeline attack,
but it’s unlikely that they were directly

00:07:07.639 --> 00:07:08.639
responsible.

00:07:08.639 --> 00:07:13.580
That’s because DarkSide is primarily focused
on developing ransomware, they leave the actual

00:07:13.580 --> 00:07:16.090
attacks up to independent hackers.

00:07:16.090 --> 00:07:20.240
This model has been called “Ransomware as
a Service” and it’s completely changing

00:07:20.240 --> 00:07:21.850
the economics of hacking.

00:07:21.850 --> 00:07:26.949
Instead of finding individual targets and
hacking them directly, DarkSide develops ransomware

00:07:26.949 --> 00:07:30.550
that other hackers can use and then turns
them loose.

00:07:30.550 --> 00:07:33.800
Just as all major tech companies have moved
from selling their software as a one time

00:07:33.800 --> 00:07:36.139
fee to now insisting on a subscription.

00:07:36.139 --> 00:07:41.080
DarkSide takes a service fee on whatever profits
are generated from using their ransomware.

00:07:41.080 --> 00:07:46.680
They typically charge 25% for ransom fees
less than $500,000, but lower that fee to

00:07:46.680 --> 00:07:49.490
10% for ransoms greater than $5 million.

00:07:49.490 --> 00:07:51.879
And there’s that $5 million dollar figure
again.

00:07:51.879 --> 00:07:57.270
It could be a coincidence, but I wouldn’t
be surprised if the hackers that use DarkSide’s

00:07:57.270 --> 00:08:00.680
Ransomware as a Service see $5 million as
a nice sweet spot.

00:08:00.680 --> 00:08:05.330
That amount is likely to be covered by insurance
and it reduces the fee paid to DarkSide for

00:08:05.330 --> 00:08:06.330
their software.

00:08:06.330 --> 00:08:10.159
The Ransomware as a Service business model
insulates DarkSide from the risk of being

00:08:10.159 --> 00:08:14.919
caught actually using these illegal tools,
but still allows them to make tons of money

00:08:14.919 --> 00:08:19.129
as armies of hackers deploy their ransomware
in companies across the world.

00:08:19.129 --> 00:08:23.530
These hackers have adopted all of the best
practices from modern tech companies and are

00:08:23.530 --> 00:08:26.939
now set to disrupt businesses at an alarming
rate.

00:08:26.939 --> 00:08:31.909
But as we’ve seen with the Colonial Pipeline
hack, these tools can have far reaching effects,

00:08:31.909 --> 00:08:34.350
often beyond what the hackers initially intended.

00:08:34.350 --> 00:08:38.280
When DarkSide lets other hackers use their
ransomware, there is a real risk that they

00:08:38.280 --> 00:08:42.760
might target the wrong organization and cause
an international incident.

00:08:42.760 --> 00:08:44.550
And this has happened before:

00:08:44.550 --> 00:08:50.030
39 hospital trusts and GPs in Scotland and
across England have had to cancel routine

00:08:50.030 --> 00:08:51.410
operations, send patients home.

00:08:51.410 --> 00:08:57.160
Cyber security experts are warning that the
rogue regime could be behind that global cyberattack

00:08:57.160 --> 00:09:01.950
that held at least 300,000 computers hostage
in over 150 countries.

00:09:01.950 --> 00:09:07.440
In 2017, North Korean hackers launched the
WannaCry ransomware attack, which targeted

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:10.400
older computers that hadn’t been patched
recently.

00:09:10.400 --> 00:09:15.790
Unfortunately, a lot of those older computers
were used in hospitals, like the United Kingdom’s

00:09:15.790 --> 00:09:17.280
National Health Service.

00:09:17.280 --> 00:09:21.850
Shutting down computers in a hospital can
have life-threatening effects, and some hospitals

00:09:21.850 --> 00:09:24.800
had to turn away ambulances during the attack.

00:09:24.800 --> 00:09:29.290
Hacks like that have real-world impact, and
draw way more attention than merely demanding

00:09:29.290 --> 00:09:31.320
payment for some corporate files.

00:09:31.320 --> 00:09:35.191
When DarkSide began selling their ransomware,
they made it clear that they didn’t want

00:09:35.191 --> 00:09:39.590
to draw too much negative attention, and even
went so far as to issue a statement saying:

00:09:39.590 --> 00:09:43.530
“that they would not target hospitals, schools,
or governments.”

00:09:43.530 --> 00:09:48.580
Cyber attacks generally fall into two categories:
espionage and sabotage.

00:09:48.580 --> 00:09:52.550
Espionage involves sneaking into a system
and stealing information, or in the case of

00:09:52.550 --> 00:09:56.720
ransomware attacks, stealing data and then
encrypting everything.

00:09:56.720 --> 00:10:00.770
Sabotage is much more aggressive, since it
involves actually trying to cause physical

00:10:00.770 --> 00:10:02.920
damage to equipment or buildings.

00:10:02.920 --> 00:10:07.890
Sabotage focused hacks are usually perpetrated
by governments, since once you destroy your

00:10:07.890 --> 00:10:11.350
targets equipment, there isn’t much financial
gain to be had.

00:10:11.350 --> 00:10:15.130
But sabotage and espionage are increasingly
starting to overlap.

00:10:15.130 --> 00:10:19.291
Sometimes hackers break into a company’s
network in order to just steal data, but wind

00:10:19.291 --> 00:10:22.220
up crippling critical infrastructure in the
process.

00:10:22.220 --> 00:10:24.870
And that’s exactly what happened with the
Colonial Pipeline.

00:10:24.870 --> 00:10:29.850
The hackers seem to have been entirely money
motivated and focused just on the ransomware

00:10:29.850 --> 00:10:34.270
scheme, but because of how various systems
are connected within Colonial’s network,

00:10:34.270 --> 00:10:38.170
the company had no choice but to shut the
pipeline down as a precaution.

00:10:38.170 --> 00:10:42.390
See, more and more, complex machinery is being
connected to computers through what are called

00:10:42.390 --> 00:10:44.320
industrial control systems.

00:10:44.320 --> 00:10:48.650
Instead of turning valves manually, computers
are now used to flip switches and save human

00:10:48.650 --> 00:10:49.980
operators time.

00:10:49.980 --> 00:10:53.850
But connecting these industrial machines to
computers means, of course, that they can

00:10:53.850 --> 00:10:59.260
be hacked, and there is one particularly interesting
case where a state actor was able to sabotage

00:10:59.260 --> 00:11:02.040
physical infrastructure using only code.

00:11:02.040 --> 00:11:07.200
It was December of 2015, and an elite team
of Russian hackers known as “Sandworm”

00:11:07.200 --> 00:11:09.720
had set their sights on Ukraine’s power
grid.

00:11:09.720 --> 00:11:14.780
They broke into the network using phishing
emails to steal administrator passwords, and,

00:11:14.780 --> 00:11:19.740
once they were in, they started remotely disabling
power substations to cause blackouts.

00:11:19.740 --> 00:11:24.570
This was the first time that a power grid
had ever been successfully targeted in a cyberattack,

00:11:24.570 --> 00:11:26.610
and it had some pretty significant consequences.

00:11:26.610 --> 00:11:31.400
As you can imagine, turning off the power
can cause real harm to people in the affected

00:11:31.400 --> 00:11:32.470
area.

00:11:32.470 --> 00:11:36.330
Hospitals and critical life support equipment
can go offline and people can die.

00:11:36.330 --> 00:11:40.820
Fortunately, in this case, workers from the
Ukrainian power companies were able to manually

00:11:40.820 --> 00:11:45.640
override the malicious code and restore power,
but you can see how these acts of sabotage

00:11:45.640 --> 00:11:47.420
can have a real impact.

00:11:47.420 --> 00:11:50.250
DarkSide wants to avoid these types of incidents.

00:11:50.250 --> 00:11:54.580
Extorting a few million dollars from a big
company makes for much milder headlines than

00:11:54.580 --> 00:11:59.510
shutting down a hospital and they know that
sabotage attacks will lead to an international

00:11:59.510 --> 00:12:00.510
response.

00:12:00.510 --> 00:12:05.060
But even though they try and sell their ransomware
only to hackers who will use it exclusively

00:12:05.060 --> 00:12:08.820
for financial gain, there is always the risk
of cascading effects.

00:12:08.820 --> 00:12:11.520
We are going to see more ransomware attacks.

00:12:11.520 --> 00:12:16.410
The technology is simply too easy to deploy
and the probability of real financial gain

00:12:16.410 --> 00:12:18.140
is simply too high.

00:12:18.140 --> 00:12:23.280
Hackers will continue to target companies
and these hacks will have unintended effects.

00:12:23.280 --> 00:12:24.710
So what’s the solution?

00:12:24.710 --> 00:12:29.930
The best approach will be multi-faceted: First,
the US needs to pressure foreign governments

00:12:29.930 --> 00:12:31.890
to stop protecting hackers.

00:12:31.890 --> 00:12:36.930
Second, we all need to improve our cybersecurity,
which means recruiting more technologists

00:12:36.930 --> 00:12:37.930
into the field.

00:12:37.930 --> 00:12:42.920
At the time of the hack, the Colonial Pipeline
had multiple cybersecurity related job openings,

00:12:42.920 --> 00:12:45.380
but it looks like they were unable to fill
them.

00:12:45.380 --> 00:12:49.610
If you’re interested in cybersecurity, now
is a great time to get into the field, I can

00:12:49.610 --> 00:12:52.770
guarantee you there will be a lot more work
to do in the future.

00:12:52.770 --> 00:12:56.830
If you want to chat about the Colonial Pipeline
hack, just leave me a comment below or DM

00:12:56.830 --> 00:13:02.020
me on Twitter, and please check out this recommended
video, the YouTube algorithm thinks you’ll

00:13:02.020 --> 00:13:02.490
really like it.

